  "title": "Conmigo, Contigo --- The Word That Says With Twice",
  "drivablePrefix": 1,
  "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:4b8e516ee25c0421",
  "lessons": [
    {
      "id": "ES-C64-conmigo",
      "sequence": 2470,
      "title": "conmigo, contigo — the word that says \"with\" twice",
      "headword": "conmigo · contigo",
      "romanization": "conmigo · contigo",
      "gloss": "with me, with you — two words carrying the same preposition twice, because the first one stopped being audible",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:1b26b60d28dcbfc0",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Para mí, para ti. The pattern is steady: preposition, then the stressed pronoun."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One preposition refuses to follow it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart: conmigo",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Con means with. But with me is not con mí:"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "¿Vienes conmigo? — Are you coming with me?"
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Here is where it came from, and it is one of the best stories in the language. Latin did not put cum in front of a pronoun; it stuck it on the end: mecum, literally me-with."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Spanish inherited that as -migo — and by then nobody could hear a preposition in it any more. It had become a meaningless tail. So Spanish put con back on the front, where prepositions belong."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Which means conmigo contains with twice: once at each end, in two different languages, fifteen centuries apart. And contigo does exactly the same."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "culture-pragmatics",
          "title": "Why it's said this way",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "This is the same instinct you saw turn mucho into muy and a el into al, running the other way. Spanish reshapes small words to keep them audible — usually by wearing them down, and here, once, by doubling one that had worn away to nothing."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "There are only two of these. From él onward the ordinary pattern returns: con él, con ella."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "conmigo, contigo, then con él",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *conmigo*, *contigo*, then *con él*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "repeat",
              "times": 2,
              "source": "[REPEAT x2]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "activity",
              "scored": true,
              "id": "ES-C64-conmigo-ask",
              "prompt": "Ask: are you coming with me?",
              "assesses": [
                "ES-LEX-CONMIGO"
              ],
              "responseSeconds": 12,
              "acceptedResponses": [
                "vienes conmigo",
                "¿vienes conmigo?"
              ],
              "feedback": {
                "correct": "Not con mi -- conmigo.",
                "incorrect": "Vienes conmigo?"
              }
            },
            {
              "kind": "activity",
              "scored": true,
              "id": "ES-C64-conmigo-twice",
              "prompt": "How many times does the word with appear inside conmigo?",
              "assesses": [
                "ES-ETYMON-CONMIGO-MECUM"
              ],
              "responseSeconds": 14,
